#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175838 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175987 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "11d9077c-a0ac-5d42-9616-b8fe867d1db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9076" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ffaf242238a8d32ce0f134cff6e0cf4256b2888b0a71f65e3c5ec470cbce284a.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}, \\: e^{y/3} + 3x \\right\\rangle.$ \nFind a repeated integral in plane polar coordinates that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\, .$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left( e^{y/3} + 3x \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e4/e4c7913793a4b08916a832e575c5b2c167d5dfeec244eea23857731df1e514e7.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}$ and $Q(x,y)=e^{y/3} + 3x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x+5}))\\,dx+({e}^{y/3}+3x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175878 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176023 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2eca638-7c7b-51ed-99e3-3b3156d13669" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "1ee74b2f-adff-5c38-8b68-753b1c02f91c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9550" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e12a47b603d2c4193453d0f4daf18f54e52ed973498f520feef32cfafbe28a9f.png)\n\n  \n\nEvaluate ![](images/69/696a0cf0c1864bfb945b4321a81525094319c9aa23491929e11dcd2b4bf2e842.png) where the path ![](images/41/41f045554bfb2c134d481db3d019376c7427e136ebd890f5537789d0c04be67d.png) is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n![](images/84/84bdabd8bc9134d577c789d22aa75d8df2b13646db10e6af742a9eba2bd0c6eb.png)\n\nIn our case, the region ![](images/28/28390f00cdb34a228868c36035ff66f1ea65bf7ff5e592fa473fca18d6addbcc.png) and its boundary ![](images/54/54c5a03048971806da4fc3eb5f51c9fdefb00abbb4a54b7bf9b94ba81ab0df37.png) satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/67/6706cc6b412a98761f0f3cd29e584f1705fa8a708a926b805a8b5702a4f508e8.png)\n\nNow, since\n![](images/13/133135fbf95e74d572c8def38a8aea6c0ef3b2e2b569a197a9c48cccd906ef44.png)\nwe obtain\n\n![](images/25/253fa3e9cb25a6b92237374e3fbc51f7e3c10461a183c789cdb37a4c4b8f560e.png)\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n![](images/cc/cce6516260d79ee10d0343f341da2b8246ca12c5e9a8ee119d8a25a29476a281.png)\n\nNow, notice that the region ![](images/05/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n![](images/5b/5b07149309edcf9c83af0282b7b8e34942ed3e6879d61f549317357d05748a45.png)\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n![](images/53/530d94860e23561ee4f296c5007cbe30a1d22028deb60fc11cb87cf5d3640497.png)\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n![](images/c8/c8094d11eab9d06ae1997a2168ec694066f59ea16aa1ec0c4788978012e62022.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175879 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "40a301b7-9eaa-59e1-bcb0-4a35fecbff69" :question/math-academy-id "e-9551" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/5b/5bf7df5fffeecf9bdaa888ebe196a194a6b6e24aa69c01ea74ee2aac1a3f4774.png)\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle y^2+\\sin x, \\: 3xy-\\cos y  \\right\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (y^2+\\sin x) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + (3xy-\\cos y) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= y^2+\\sin x$ and $Q(x,y)= 3xy-\\cos y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dx+(xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0\\leq r \\leq 2,\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi  \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $r^2\\sin\\theta.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176023 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2eca638-7c7b-51ed-99e3-3b3156d13669" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6cb8fa1-2009-59ba-aea5-fdafb959f4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173645} {:answer/id #uuid "d2584ead-6ce1-5142-b0b7-7db938b54472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173646} {:answer/id #uuid "db8872af-a4b1-505f-9bb4-05d86abc0f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173647} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac00681-d43e-5de5-8a5a-bb36931810eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173648} {:answer/id #uuid "220a29a6-9c6b-5aa5-9adb-2a7ceef7a3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173649}] :answer-field/id #uuid "649091b6-9ce8-5c4a-990c-40e71de247f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173644}] :question/id #uuid "ac600244-dbff-596b-9fbb-094ec0801e81" :question/math-academy-id "q-109119" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $∮_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = ⟨x^{2}y - \\cos x, 4 - xy^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$, oriented counterclockwise."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b458066d-efc5-5560-ba22-4506001a9557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r - r^{2}\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175049} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5a672a-4af5-59ce-adcb-ec50c39fb584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin θ - r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175050} {:answer/id #uuid "d992dec3-0692-5ca6-b4ca-b24febe7be95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r - 2r^{2}\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175051} {:answer/id #uuid "10408896-e2a5-5308-aa63-b0c896832fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - 2r\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175052} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8e6021-b1fd-5e20-a95f-21fc240788a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r - r^{2}\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175053}] :answer-field/id #uuid "982dc660-32e3-5583-8321-c1dc1afc1069" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175048}] :question/id #uuid "bdd86cce-9e5b-5a18-9af7-54a7f6efd03e" :question/math-academy-id "q-41469" :question/problem "Given that\n$∮_(C)y^{2}dx + xdy = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}AAAAAAAAdrdθ$,\nwhere $C$ is the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?"}]}
